======================================================================
  SYNTHESIS: ISO CLASS GRAPH AS REEB-LIKE QUOTIENT
======================================================================

  The iso class graph at n=5 is a REFINEMENT of the Reeb graph:
  - Reeb graph has 306 nodes (one per H-level component)
  - Iso class graph has 12 nodes (one per iso class)
  - At H=3: Reeb has 1 component, iso has 3 classes
  - At H=9: Reeb has 1 component, iso has 2 classes

  The almost-DAG structure (29 up, 0 down, 1 level) follows from:
  1. Walsh even-order pattern (H is complement-invariant)
  2. Order-2 dominance (94.7% => score determines H direction)
  3. The single level edge (at H=9) is where order-4 cancels order-2

  The blue/black coloring adds ORTHOGONAL information:
  - Blue (SC-preserving) edges stay on the y=x diagonal
  - Black (SC-changing) edges cross the diagonal
  - The blue subgraph has 3 components:
    1. SC classes: fully connected (8 classes)
    2-3. Non-SC classes: paired (2+2)

  CONNECTIONS TO EXTERNAL THEORY:
  - Brualdi-Li interchange: score-preserving subgraph = bipartite regular
  - Chudnovsky-Seymour WQO: H-hereditary classes have finite obstruction sets
  - Stockmeyer: non-reconstructible pairs share vertex-deck but may differ in H
  - Royle et al (2023): A000568 counts BOTH tournaments AND even graphs
  - Pachner analogy: C3-reversal = bistellar flip, both preserve 'type'

  THE FUNDAMENTAL INSIGHT:
  The iso class graph is a QUOTIENT of the tournament hypercube
  by S_n action. H descends to this quotient as a quasi-elementary
  function with no odd Fourier components. The almost-DAG property
  is the quotient image of the single-basin Morse landscape.
  Breaking it into blue (SC-diagonal) and black (off-diagonal)
  reveals the y=x symmetry-breaking structure found in S20x.
